<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Joaquín Chamorro Cardenal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Joaquín Chamorro Cardenal was a Nicaraguan journalist, editor of the newspaper La Prensa and a leading opponent of the Somoza dictatorship, who was repeatedly jailed and exiled. His assassination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> on 10 January 1978 provoked strikes, riots and a national uprising that ended with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a> in 1979. His widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/violeta-chamorro">Violeta Barrios de Chamorro</a>, became president in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-joaquin-chamorro-cardenal.jpg" alt="Pedro Joaquín Chamorro Cardenal" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Canal 12 Nicaragua · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Joaqu%C3%ADn_Chamorro_Cardenal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
